# TypeScript Style

> The **## Naming** section below is about *naming*, not TypeScript syntax, so
> it applies to **every language in this repo, Rust included** — that's why this
> rule is also globbed to `*.rs`. A Rust struct named `ProducerState`, a field
> `state: Mutex<…>`, a field `command_type`, or a local `let data = …` are all
> violations of the bans below. The rest of the guide (return types, React
> imports, hooks) is TypeScript-only; ignore it when editing `.rs`.

## Naming

### No `state` in identifiers — HARD BAN (the most-missed rule)

This ban is also restated in `code-organization.mdc` (which is always
applied) precisely because it keeps getting missed. Read it twice.

The word `state` is banned in all identifier names — variables, parameters,
fields, methods, type aliases, interfaces, Rust structs/enums. This covers
compounds like `columnState`, `tableState`, `getColumnState`, `ColumnState`,
`TableState`, `MenuState`, `setState`, `getInitialState`, `getPlayerState`, and
in Rust `ProducerState` / `state: Mutex<…>`.

Why: every value in the program is some kind of state. The name carries no
information about *what kind*. Describe the actual shape instead —
`columnLayout`, `sortModel`, `selection`, `dragSession`, `hoverInfo`,
`columnFlags`. Precedent already in the codebase: `SortModel` (formerly
`SortState`), `dragSession` (not `dragState`), `Layout` (not `LayoutState`),
`ProducerSession` (not `ProducerState`).

```ts
// ❌ "state" is noise
const columnState = getColumnState(column);
interface TableState {}
function setState(updater) {}

// ✅ Describe the shape
const flags = getColumnFlags(column);
interface TableModel {}
function applyChange(updater) {}
```

```rust
// ❌ "state" names nothing
struct ProducerState { /* events, queue, models, … */ }
struct Producer { state: Mutex<ProducerState> }

// ✅ name the live data the producer keeps for its run
struct ProducerSession { /* events, queue, models, … */ }
struct Producer { session: Mutex<ProducerSession> }
```

Narrow exception: when interfacing with an external API that literally
exports an identifier named with `state` (e.g. React's `useState`, YouTube's
`onStateChange` event key and `PlayerState` enum), keep *their* surface — you
cannot rename the key a library reads or the global it exposes. But the
exception covers the boundary only. Don't thread the word into the identifiers
we own (variables, refs, helper functions), and don't re-declare an external
method like `getPlayerState()` in our own interface when we can read the same
value another way — track a `livePlaying` ref off the events we already receive
and read that. Keep the word at the single unavoidable call, never in our logic.

The most common trap: the `useState` **hook** is the allowed external name, but
the **setter you destructure from its tuple is ours** and is fully bound by the
ban. Reaching for `setVolumeState` / `setLoopState` because the clean
`setVolume` / `setLoop` name is already taken by a public wrapper is **not** an
escape hatch — it is exactly the violation. Give the internal writer a real verb
(`storeVolume`, `applyLoop`, `putSelection`) and keep the clean name for the
surface.

```ts
// ❌ "State" smuggled onto the setter to dodge a name clash with the public fn
const [volume, setVolumeState] = React.useState(12);
const [loop, setLoopState] = React.useState(false);
const setVolume = (n: number) => { player.setVolume(n); setVolumeState(n); };
const setLoop = (on: boolean) => setLoopState(on);

// ✅ the internal writer takes a real verb; the public name stays clean
const [volume, storeVolume] = React.useState(12);
const [loop, storeLoop] = React.useState(false);
const setVolume = (n: number) => { player.setVolume(n); storeVolume(n); };
const setLoop = (on: boolean) => storeLoop(on);
```

### No `shared` in identifiers

The word `shared` is banned in all names — files, **folders**, variables,
parameters, fields, type aliases, interfaces. This covers a `shared/` folder, a
`shared.ts` file, `sharedConfig`, `getShared`, `SharedProps`. (This is the
identifier counterpart to the file/folder ban already in
`code-organization.mdc`.)

Why: `shared` names a *relationship* — "stuff more than one caller happens to
use" — not what the thing is. Everything in a module is shared by its callers,
so the word adds nothing. Name the actual bundle: a folder of reusable scrubber
pieces is `parts/`, common pure math is `scrub-math.ts`, a reused config is the
`baseConfig`. Promote a helper into a named module only once a second real
caller appears (per `code-organization.mdc`).

```ts
// ❌ "shared" names the relationship, not the thing
import { Transport } from "../shared/transport";
interface SharedProps {}

// ✅ Name what the folder / bundle actually holds
import { Transport } from "../parts/transport";
interface TransportProps {}
```

### No `type` in identifiers

The word `type` is banned in identifier names — variables, parameters, fields,
type aliases, interfaces, Rust structs/enums. This covers `eventType`,
`commandType`, `command_type`, `MessageType`, `getType`, `nodeType`,
`fileType`.

Why: `type` is the emptiest possible discriminator — *everything* has a type.
The name says "this distinguishes between kinds" without saying *which* kinds.
Name the discriminator after the domain it splits: a command has a `kind`, an
event has a `name`, a node has a `role`, a file has a `format`. (This is about
identifiers; the `type` *keyword* — `type Foo = …` in TS, `type X = …` in Rust
— is the language, not a name, and is fine.)

```ts
// ❌ "type" names the relationship, not the kind
const eventType = event.type;
interface MessageType {}

// ✅ name the actual axis it splits on
const eventName = event.name;
type Command = { kind: "subscribe" | "cancel" | "message" };
```

```rust
// ❌ a field literally called *_type
pub command_type: String,
match command.command_type.as_str() { … }

// ✅ the domain word; keep the wire name via serde rename
#[serde(rename = "type")]
pub kind: String,
match command.kind.as_str() { … }
```

Narrow exception: an external wire/JSON field literally named `type` keeps its
*serialized* name through a rename attribute (`#[serde(rename = "type")]`, a
third-party union member `{ type: "…" }`), but the identifier we own takes the
domain word. Don't introduce `type` into our identifiers.

### No `data` in identifiers

The word `data` is banned in identifier names — variables, parameters, fields,
type aliases, interfaces, Rust structs/enums. This covers `userData`,
`responseData`, `data`, `getData`, `DataModel`, `metaData`.

Why: `data` is the emptiest noun in the language — every value is data. Name
what the bundle actually holds: raw bytes headed to a socket are a `frame`,
`payload`, or `blob`; a parsed body is a `record` or `response`; a list of rows
is `rows`.

```ts
// ❌ "data" says nothing
const data = await res.json();
function getData() {}

// ✅ name what it is
const record = await res.json();
function loadRows() {}
```

```rust
// ❌ a local called data
let data = Blob::new(line.as_bytes().to_vec());
self.ws.post_to_connection().data(data).send().await

// ✅ name the bytes; the external builder method keeps its name
let frame = Blob::new(line.as_bytes().to_vec());
self.ws.post_to_connection().data(frame).send().await
```

Narrow exception: an external API surface that literally uses `data` keeps its
name — the DOM `event.data`, `FormData`, the SSE `data:` field, the AWS
`.data(…)` builder method. Don't introduce `data` into the identifiers we own.

### No `helpers` in identifiers

The word `helpers` is banned in all identifier names — variables, parameters,
fields, type aliases, interfaces. This covers `rowHelpers`, `getHelpers`,
`RowHelpers`, `ghostHelpers`. (This is the identifier counterpart to the
filename ban in `code-organization.mdc`, which already forbids `helpers.ts`.)

Why: `helpers` says "a grab-bag of stuff some other code needs" — it names the
*relationship* ("things that help X") instead of *what the thing is*. Name the
actual bundle. A set of per-row flags plus `toggle` / `select` callbacks is the
row's *affordances*; a few pure functions are the *math* or the *parse* step.
Precedent already in the codebase: `RowAffordances` (formerly `RowHelpers`),
`ColumnAffordances`, `tree-math.ts` (not `tree-helpers.ts`).

```ts
// ❌ "helpers" names the relationship, not the thing
interface RowHelpers {}
const ghostHelpers = (row: TreeRow): RowHelpers => ({ … });
function getHelpers() {}

// ✅ Name what the bundle actually is
interface RowAffordances {}
const ghostAffordances = (row: TreeRow): RowAffordances => ({ … });
```

### No `default` / `defaults` in identifiers

The words `default` and `defaults` are banned in all identifier names —
variables, parameters, fields, type aliases, interfaces, functions. This covers
`functionDefaults`, `getDefaults`, `Defaults`, `defaultConfig`, `withDefaults`.

Why: `defaults` names a grab-bag — "the fallback values for X" — instead of
*what the bundle actually is*. A const holding the shared Lambda args every
route reuses is the `functionArgs`; a config object carrying starting values is
the `baseConfig` or the specific shape it holds. Name the thing, not its
relationship to whatever overrides it.

```ts
// ❌ "defaults" names the relationship, not the thing
const functionDefaults = { environment, link, permissions };
function getDefaults() {}

// ✅ Name what the bundle actually is
const functionArgs = { environment, link, permissions };
```

Narrow exception: an external surface that literally uses the word keeps its
name — a `default` export, React's `defaultValue` prop, the API Gateway
`$default` route key. Don't introduce the word into ours.

### Never spell out `index` — use `i` or `idx`

The word `index` is banned in identifiers. Use `i` for the loop/iteration
counter (the throwaway integer in a `for` / `map` / `forEach`), and `idx`
everywhere else the value is kept and read later (a field, a stored position,
an argument). This covers `siblingIndex`, `rawIndex`, `insertIndex`,
`draggedIndex`, `getIndex`, `IndexProps`.

Why: `index` is the long form of a name the whole industry already abbreviates.
`i` reads as "the counter right here"; `idx` reads as "a position I'm keeping".
Spelling it out adds letters without adding meaning, and the two short forms
already encode *which kind* it is.

```ts
// ❌ spelled out
siblings.forEach((item, index) => { … });
const insertIndex = clamp(gapIndex, 0, len);
interface TreeRow { siblingIndex: number }

// ✅ i for the live counter, idx for a kept position
siblings.forEach((item, i) => { … });
const insertIdx = clamp(gapIdx, 0, len);
interface TreeRow { siblingIdx: number }
```

Narrow exception: an external API that hands you `index` (a library callback
param, `Array.prototype.findIndex` is fine as a method name) keeps its surface.

### One export per module file — default *or* named, never `import { default as }`

A module file exposes exactly one public thing through its `index`, and the
shape of that export is chosen by how consumers read it. If every consumer
writes `import Foo from "..."`, use `export default`. If consumers want
`import { Foo } from "..."`, use a named `export` — and then there is no
default export at all. The smell that proves you picked wrong is
`import { default as Foo }`: that is a consumer asking for a *named* import
from something you exported as default, so it should have been a named export
from the start.

```ts
// ❌ both, or a default that consumers rename to a name
export function Tree() {}
export default Tree;                       // two exports
import { default as Tree } from "...";     // consumer wants it named — so name it

// ✅ pick one based on the call site
export default function Tree() {}           // consumers: import Tree from "..."
// — or —
export function Tree() {}                   // consumers: import { Tree } from "..."
```

Components and hooks still prefer `export default function Name` (per
`module-structure`) when consumed as a default; the rule here is just: never
ship both, and never force a `default as` rename.

### Prefer `Array.from` / `.concat` over spread for arrays

Default to `Array.from(x)` to materialize an iterable and `a.concat(b)` to join
arrays, instead of `[...x]` / `[...a, ...b]`. There is no meaningful runtime
difference (`concat` is typically equal-or-faster than a two-spread literal;
`Array.from` and `[...x]` are equivalent), so this is a readability default, not
a perf claim.

```ts
// ❌ spread
const copy = [...set];
const all = [...a, ...b];
const ids = [...new Set([...current, id])];

// ✅ from / concat
const copy = Array.from(set);
const all = a.concat(b);
const ids = Array.from(new Set(current).add(id));
```

The one allowed exception is *typing friction*: if `.concat` forces a manual
type annotation or a cast to compile (mixed-element tuples, union narrowing),
prefer the spread that compiles cleanly over a hand-typed `concat`. Spread to
avoid a cast, never to save a character.

### Reach for modern Baseline APIs

Prefer the built-in modern method over the hand-rolled equivalent. The ones
that come up most here:

- `map.getOrInsert(key, [])` instead of `const v = map.get(key) ?? []; map.set(key, v)`.
- `array.toSorted(cmp)` / `toReversed()` / `with(i, v)` instead of copy-then-mutate.
- `Set` ops — `a.union(b)`, `a.intersection(b)`, `a.difference(b)` — instead of
  manual filter loops.
- `Object.groupBy` / `Map.groupBy` instead of a reduce-into-buckets.
- `array.at(-1)` instead of `array[array.length - 1]`.

The full catalog lives in the **`baseline-js`** skill — read it when writing or
auditing JS/TS/React. The CSS counterpart is the **`baseline-css`** skill. The
test is simple: if a feature is **Baseline**, use it (this app ships on
WKWebView/JavaScriptCore, so "works in Safari" == "works for us"); if it isn't
Baseline, don't reach for it. `Map.prototype.getOrInsert` is Baseline and is
confirmed working in our engine, so use it freely.

### Use `Temporal`, not `Date` — HARD BAN on `Date` in app code

All date/time work uses `Temporal`. **`Date` is banned in the code we own** — no
`new Date(...)`, no `Date.now()`, no `Date.UTC(...)`, and none of the `Date`
instance methods (`.getHours()`, `.getTime()`, `.toLocaleString()`, …). Temporal
is immutable, explicit about the time zone, and typechecks here
(`lib.esnext.temporal` ships in our TS lib).

The polyfill is **already wired**: `import "temporal-polyfill/global"` is the
first line of `atlas/index.tsx`, so `Temporal` is a real, typed global at
runtime. Use it directly — no import, exactly like `day-timeline.tsx` and
`format-time.ts` already do. (`Temporal` is not Baseline yet, hence the
polyfill; if you ever stand up a new entry point, install the polyfill there
before the first `Temporal` use or it compiles green and throws.)

The exact swaps — these are the ones that keep getting reached for out of habit:

```ts
// ❌ Date                          →  ✅ Temporal
Date.now()                          →  Temporal.Now.instant().epochMilliseconds
new Date(ms)                        →  Temporal.Instant.fromEpochMilliseconds(ms)
new Date(isoString)                 →  Temporal.Instant.from(isoString)   // needs an offset / Z
d.getHours() / d.getMinutes()       →  zdt.hour / zdt.minute
d.getFullYear() / d.getDate()       →  zdt.year / zdt.day                 // Temporal months are 1-based
```

To read or display an instant you almost always want a `ZonedDateTime`, because
the zone has to be explicit. Convert epoch-ms to the user's local zone (the
common case), or build from parts in a fixed zone:

```ts
// epoch ms → the user's local wall clock
Temporal.Instant.fromEpochMilliseconds(ms).toZonedDateTimeISO(Temporal.Now.timeZoneId());
// broken-down parts in a known zone — months are 1-based, so NO `- 1`
Temporal.ZonedDateTime.from({ timeZone: "UTC", year, month, day, hour, minute });
```

Precedent: `weather/format-time.ts` exports `localTime(ms)` and `utcTime(ms)`
that do exactly this, and every weather tooltip/overlay reads `Temporal.Now`.
Reach for those helpers (or copy their shape) instead of building a `Date`.

The **only** allowed `Date` is one an external library forces on us — e.g.
`satellite.js`'s `propagate(satrec, date)` / `gstime(date)` take a legacy JS
`Date`. Convert at that single boundary and keep `Date` out of everything else:

```ts
// ✅ Date confined to the satellite.js call; Temporal everywhere around it
const groundPositions = (sats: Satellite[], at: Temporal.Instant) => {
  const atDate = new Date(at.epochMilliseconds); // satellite.js needs a JS Date
  const gmst = gstime(atDate);
  // …propagate(satrec, atDate)…
};
```

If you catch yourself typing `new Date` or `Date.now()` for anything other than
that kind of forced external boundary, stop and use `Temporal`.

### No `Use` prefix on hook return / option types

Hook function names start with `use` per React's calling-convention. The
*types* associated with the hook must not carry that prefix. Drop it.

```ts
// ❌ Verb form leaking into a noun
export type UseColumnAffordancesResult<T> = ReturnType<typeof useColumnAffordances<T>>;
export interface UseTableProps {}
export interface UseFooOptions {}

// ✅ Plain nouns
export type ColumnAffordances<T> = ReturnType<typeof useColumnAffordances<T>>;
export interface TableProps {}
export interface FooOptions {}
```

Why: `use` is a calling-convention marker for the React hook lint rule, not
a semantic part of what the *value* is. `UseFooResult` reads as a verb
phrase used where a noun belongs. Precedent: `Controller` (returned by
`usePanels`), `Layout`, `PanelSize`.

### Exported type names

Exported TypeScript type aliases and interfaces use descriptive domain
names. No leading `T` prefix. Prefer names that explain the role of the
shape, such as `TableModel`, `SortModel`, `ColumnSizing`, or `UploadDraft`.

```ts
// ✅ Domain names
export interface TableModel {
  activeRowId: RowId | null;
  columnSizing: ColumnSizing;
}

export interface SortModel {
  columnId: ColumnId;
  direction: SortDirection;
}

// ❌ Prefixes
export interface TColumn {}
```

## No Explicit Return Types

Let TypeScript infer return types. Don't annotate them unless necessary for documentation or type narrowing.

```ts
// ✅ Let TS infer
const getUser = (id: string) => users.get(id);
const formatTime = (seconds: number) => `${seconds}s`;
const findItem = (id: string) => items.find((i) => i.id === id);

// ❌ Redundant explicit return types
const getUser = (id: string): User | undefined => users.get(id);
const formatTime = (seconds: number): string => `${seconds}s`;
```

## When Explicit Return Types ARE Needed

Only use explicit return types when:

1. **Public API contracts** - Exported functions in shared packages where the return type is part of the API contract
2. **Recursion** - a function that calls itself genuinely can't have its return type inferred; annotate those
3. **Complex inference** - When TS can't infer correctly (rare)
4. **Intentional narrowing** - When you want to return a narrower type than inferred

When a call site needs a different or narrower type than the body infers, prefer a
cast at the `return` (`return rows as Foo[]`) over annotating the whole signature —
the cast keeps the type assertion on the one line that needs it instead of pinning
the function's surface. Reserve the signature annotation for the cases above.

```ts
// ✅ OK: Intentional narrowing for API contract
export const createId = (): string => nanoid();

// ✅ OK: Complex generic that needs help
const merge = <T>(a: T, b: Partial<T>): T => ({ ...a, ...b });
```

## Prefer `const` arrow functions over `function` declarations

Default to `const name = (...) => ...` for every function you write. The
`function` keyword is reserved for React components and hooks
(`export function RadarMap()`, `export default function useThing()`), which read
best as declarations (see `code-organization.mdc`). Everything else — exported or
private, sync or async, a data fetcher, an overlay installer, a pure helper — is a
`const` arrow.

```ts
// ✅ const arrows
export const fetchRadarFrames = async () => { … };
const installNwsAlerts = async (ctx: OverlayContext) => { … };
const offsetLabel = (time: number, now: number) => { … };

// ❌ bare function declarations for non-components
export async function fetchRadarFrames() { … }
async function installNwsAlerts(ctx) { … }
function offsetLabel(time, now) { … }
```

A `const` helper may sit **below** the main export that calls it. `const` is not
hoisted, but that does not matter here: the main export is only *called* at runtime
(React renders the component, or a consumer imports and invokes the function), long
after the module finished evaluating top-to-bottom. By call time every
module-level `const` below has already initialized, so the closure resolves. So
"main thing first, helpers under it" reads top-down and still works with `const`.

```ts
// ✅ component on top, const helpers underneath — fine, they only run on render
export function Scrubber(props: Props) {
  return <Block>{offsetLabel(props.t, props.now)}</Block>;
}
const offsetLabel = (t: number, now: number) => { … };
```

The **only** time the `function` keyword is required for a non-component helper is
when it must be reachable somewhere a `const` can't be — almost always a helper
written *after* a component's `return`. Code after `return` never runs during
render, so a `const` there is never initialized; a hoisted `function` declaration
is. If you don't need that hoist, put the helper above the return as a `const`.

```ts
// ✅ acceptable: helper after the return needs the hoist, so it's a `function`
export function Strip(props: Props) {
  return <Block>{hourLabel(props.t)}</Block>;
  function hourLabel(t: number) { … } // a `const` here would be undefined at render
}
```

## Parameter Types

Always type function parameters - these cannot be inferred:

```ts
// ✅ Parameters must be typed
const greet = (name: string, age: number) => `${name} is ${age}`;

// ❌ Missing parameter types
const greet = (name, age) => `${name} is ${age}`;
```

## Arguments: assign the object, destructure in the body

Never destructure an object argument in the parameter position. Take the whole
object as one named parameter, then destructure on the first line of the body.
The signature stays a single typed parameter, the destructure sits right next to
the fields it pulls, and you can read `props.x` directly when that is clearer.

```ts
// ❌ Destructured in the argument position
export const List = ({ rows, activeId, onPlay }: Props) => (
  // ...
);

// ✅ Assign the object, then destructure in the body
export const List = (props: Props) => {
  const { rows, activeId, onPlay } = props;
  // ...
};
```

For one or two fields where a body destructure is noise, just read them off the
object (`props.size`). The only hard rule is no destructuring in the parameter
list itself. This is about the argument *object*: destructuring a known tuple
inside a callback (`useTransform([a, b], ([x, y]) => …)`) is fine.

## Import React as a namespace

Import React as a namespace and reach for hooks and React types through it.
Don't destructure named hooks from `"react"`.

```ts
// ❌ Named imports
import { useState, useRef } from "react";
const [open, setOpen] = useState(false);

// ✅ Namespace
import * as React from "react";
const [open, setOpen] = React.useState(false);
```

One namespace import covers every hook and React type a file touches, so adding
`React.useEffect` or `React.PointerEvent` never means editing the import line,
and the `React.` prefix reads unambiguously at the call site. For a file that
only needs React *types*, `import type * as React from "react"` keeps it out of
the runtime bundle. Library hooks (framer-motion, tanstack) keep their own named
imports — this is about React's surface only.

## Consolidate related state

Look for chances to collapse several pieces of related state into one. Booleans
that always move together, or an id plus the object it points at, are usually
one model wearing three hats. Prefer, in order:

- A **string union** when the thing is really one of N modes
  (`type Phase = "idle" | "open" | "closing"`), not three booleans.
- A **`useReducer`** when a few related fields change together through a small
  set of named transitions — the reducer names the transitions and keeps invalid
  combinations unrepresentable.
- **immer** (`produce`, already a dependency) inside a reducer when the shape is
  deeply nested and spreading every level (`{ ...a, b: { ...a.b, c } }`) is the
  only thing making the update hard to read.

```ts
// ❌ Three useStates that always move together
const [activeId, setActiveId] = React.useState<string | null>(null);
const [detailRow, setDetailRow] = React.useState<Row | null>(null);
const [playingId, setPlayingId] = React.useState<string | null>(null);

// ✅ One reducer that names the transitions
const [view, dispatch] = React.useReducer(reduce, initialView);
dispatch({ type: "openDetail", row });
```

Don't over-rotate: two unrelated booleans are fine as two booleans. The trigger
is *related* state — fields set, cleared, or read together.

## Never the `void` operator (HARD BAN)

`void somePromiseFn()` is banned. Fire-and-forget is spelled as a plain call:
`somePromiseFn()`. The operator adds a keyword that says nothing the call
doesn't already say — we don't use a floating-promises lint rule, so there is
nothing to appease. Oxlint's `no-void` (already `error` in every package)
enforces this; never weaken that rule to readmit it.

```ts
// Bad — ceremonial keyword.
void runScan(follow.id);

// Good — just call it.
runScan(follow.id);
```

(The `void` *type* in annotations like `() => void` is TypeScript and fine —
this ban is about the expression operator.)

## Event listeners: one `AbortController` signal

When you attach DOM listeners imperatively, pass an `AbortController` signal and
call `controller.abort()` to remove them — don't name each handler just so you
can hand the same reference back to `removeEventListener`. One `abort()` tears
down every listener wired to the signal, so there is no handler to keep around
(the self-referencing `const stop = () => removeEventListener("pointerup", stop)`
shape is the smell this replaces).

```ts
// ❌ Named handlers kept only so they can be removed
const move = (e: PointerEvent) => onMove(e);
const stop = () => {
  window.removeEventListener("pointermove", move);
  window.removeEventListener("pointerup", stop);
};
window.addEventListener("pointermove", move);
window.addEventListener("pointerup", stop);

// ✅ One signal removes them all
const controller = new AbortController();
const { signal } = controller;
window.addEventListener("pointermove", (e) => onMove(e), { signal });
window.addEventListener("pointerup", () => controller.abort(), { signal });
```

In a `useEffect`, create the controller in the effect body and return
`() => controller.abort()` as the cleanup — one line removes whatever it attached.

## Memoization (`useCallback` / `useMemo`)

**HARD DEFAULT: write plain functions and inline object/array literals. Do
NOT reach for `useCallback` or `useMemo`.** This codebase runs React 19
with the React Compiler enabled — the compiler memoizes everything it can
see automatically. Wrapping for "perf", "stable reference", "to avoid
re-renders", or "to keep the context value stable" is dead weight and
noise; the compiler already did it. Delete the wrapper.

If you are about to type `React.useCallback` or `React.useMemo`, STOP.
Re-read the exception list below. If your case is not on it (and you
cannot add a comment naming a specific concrete consumer that needs the
stable identity), write a plain function or inline literal instead.

Common bad reflexes that this rule forbids:

- "I'll memoize this context value so consumers don't re-render." — No.
  The compiler memoizes the value object. Write `const value = { … };`.
- "This handler is passed to a child, I'll `useCallback` it." — No.
  The compiler memoizes it. Pass the arrow function directly.
- "I want a stable reference for cleanliness." — Not a reason.

`useCallback` / `useMemo` are permitted only when there is a **concrete
identity-stability requirement the compiler cannot satisfy**. Typical cases:

- Dependency of a `useEffect` / `useLayoutEffect` deps array, where a
  fresh identity each render would re-fire the effect.
- Passed as a React `ref` callback (`<div ref={fn} />` or returned from
  a hook that is consumed as a ref). React fires `(oldRef, null)` then
  `(newRef, element)` on every render where the function reference
  changes — identity churn re-runs the ref body every render and
  infinite-loops any `setState` it triggers.
- Identity that is read by an imperatively attached listener
  (`addEventListener`, `ResizeObserver`, `IntersectionObserver`, etc.)
  whose attach/detach is keyed off the function reference.
- Passed into a memoized child or library API that compares by reference
  (e.g. `virtualizer.measureElement`, a `React.memo` boundary the compiler
  cannot inline through, a third-party hook).
- Referenced by a `useImperativeHandle` whose identity propagates to a
  parent `ref`.
- Object/array passed as a context value where consumers re-render on
  reference change (the compiler bails out on most provider bodies that
  touch refs / nested closures / observers — when in doubt, wrap the
  context value in `useMemo` with explicit deps).

**Every retained `useCallback` / `useMemo` must have a one-line comment
immediately above it that names the specific consumer requiring the
stable identity** — per `documentation-comments.mdc`, the comment names
the concrete consumer, not a generic justification.

```ts
// ❌ Throat-clearing — banned
// useCallback needed on React 19 with compiler because the compiler cannot see across this boundary
// memoize for performance
// stable reference
const handleClick = React.useCallback(() => {
  doThing();
}, []);

// ❌ No comment at all
const handleClick = React.useCallback(() => {
  doThing();
}, []);

// ❌ Wrapper exists but nothing actually needs the stable identity
const handleClick = React.useCallback(() => {
  doThing(value);
}, [value]);

return <button onClick={handleClick}>Go</button>;

// ✅ Names the concrete consumer and what would break without it
// dep of the resize-observer effect; identity must outlive re-renders
const measure = React.useCallback(() => {
  setWidth(ref.current?.clientWidth ?? 0);
}, []);

React.useEffect(() => {
  const observer = new ResizeObserver(measure);
  observer.observe(ref.current!);
  return () => observer.disconnect();
}, [measure]);

// ✅ Names the consumer and the comparison contract
// passed into virtualizer.measureElement which compares by reference
const measureElement = React.useCallback(
  (el: Element | null) => virtualizer.measureElement(el),
  [virtualizer]
);
```

When in doubt, drop the wrapper. If something downstream actually needs
the stable identity, it will become obvious — and you can add the
wrapper back with the comment that explains why.
